Proposition 2.1 states that, in a partially ordered set with the induced topology, a monotone net which has a least upper bound converges to \(y\) if and only if \(y\) lies below that least upper bound. `proposition_2_1` proves the same biconditional for a monotone net in a complete lattice: `ScottConvergesTo` holds exactly when \(y \le L\), with \(L\) a least upper bound of the range, where convergence means eventual membership in every `ScottOpen` neighbourhood and `ScottOpen` is an upper set inaccessible by nonempty directed suprema. `proposition_2_1_le_of_converges` is the forward half: if \(y \nleq L\), convergence into the Scott-open set of elements not below \(L\) yields a value of the net, and the upper-bound half of the least-upper-bound property puts that value below \(L\). `proposition_2_1_of_le` is the backward half: monotonicity makes the range directed, \(y \le L\) and the upper-set clause put the supremum of the range into any Scott-open neighbourhood of \(y\), and inaccessibility supplies a value already in the neighbourhood, after which monotonicity keeps the tail inside. The note states this for an arbitrary poset in which the lub of the net exists. Every gloss assumes a complete lattice. **Theorem:** partial — the inequality \(y \sqsubseteq \bigsqcup_i x_i\) is the biconditional of `proposition_2_1`, proved only for complete lattices. **Proof:** yes — the two directional glosses are the forward and backward arguments, and the note has no separate proof text to compare.
